.TH PMM 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
pmm_alloc_block, pmm_free_block, pmm_alloc_contiguous, pmm_free_contiguous, pmm_watermark_alloc \- physical memory manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/arch/i386/pmm.h>
.LP
.BI "void *pmm_alloc_block(void);"
.br
.BI "void pmm_free_block(void *" p ");"
.br
.BI "void *pmm_alloc_contiguous(size_t " count ");"
.br
.BI "void pmm_free_contiguous(void *" p ", size_t " count ");"
.br
.BI "void *pmm_watermark_alloc(size_t " bytes ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Physical Memory Manager (PMM) manages the allocation and release of physical memory frames (4KB blocks). It employs a hybrid strategy using free lists for O(1) single-page allocations and a Buddy Allocator for O(log N) contiguous allocations.
.SS Early Boot Allocation
.B pmm_watermark_alloc
allocates memory from a reserved early-boot region ("watermark") before the main allocator is initialized. This memory cannot be freed. It returns a virtual address in the kernel higher-half (0xC0000000+).
.SS Standard Allocation
.B pmm_alloc_block
allocates a single 4KB physical page. It returns the physical address of the page, or NULL if no memory is available.
.PP
.B pmm_free_block
releases a single 4KB physical page back to the pool.
.I p
must be a valid physical address previously allocated.
.PP
.B pmm_alloc_contiguous
allocates
.I count
physically contiguous pages. This uses the Buddy Allocator to find a suitable block.
.PP
.B pmm_free_contiguous
releases a contiguous block of
.I count
pages.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
The allocator uses per-order free lists (orders 0-10) to manage blocks from 4KB to 4MB.
.IP \[bu]
A bitmap is maintained for diagnostics but is not the primary allocation mechanism.
.IP \[bu]
.B pmm_init
must be called during kernel bootstrap to populate the free lists from the memory map (e.g., E820).
.SH RETURN VALUES
.B pmm_alloc_block
and
.B pmm_alloc_contiguous
return the physical address of the allocated memory, or NULL on failure.
.B pmm_watermark_alloc
returns a kernel virtual address.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pmap (9),
.BR vm_page (9)
